We consider the published distance moduli to the Fornax and Coma galaxy clusters, with emphasis on the period since 1990. We have carefully homogenized our catalogs of distance moduli onto the distance scale established in the previous papers in this series. We assessed systematic differences associated with the use of specific tracers, and discarded results based on application of the Tully--Fisher relation and of globular cluster and planetary nebula luminosity functions. We recommend `best' weighted relative distance moduli for the Fornax and Coma clusters with respect to the Virgo cluster of $Δ(m-M)_0^{m Fornax - Virgo} = 0.18 \pm 0.28 $ mag and $Δ(m-M)_0^{m Coma - Virgo} = 3.75 \pm 0.23$ mag. The set of weighted mean distance moduli (distances) we derived as most representative of the clusters' distances is,  egin{eqnarray} (m-M)_0^{m Fornax} &=& 31.41 \pm 0.15 \mbox{ mag } (D = 19.1^{+1.4}_{-1.2} \mbox{ Mpc) and} 
